  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 138 AM CST Mon Nov 23 2026\n\nConditions more typical for late November are in store for the \nremainder of the month. Ongoing CAA in the wake of Sunday's cold\nfront will result in a somewhat blustery day today, with highs \nstruggling to reach around 40F. As a surface ridge nears from \nthe northwest overnight, the combination of a cold start to the \nnight and a very dry airmass will result in what will likely be \nour coldest night so far this season. Though thickening upper-\nlevel cloud cover will spread in from the west late, the coldest\nlocations in interior northern Illinois will likely see temps \nfalling into at least the upper teens. Meanwhile, marginal \nSST-850 hPa delta temps over Lake Michigan and a strong \ninversion around 6kft may be sufficient to generate plenty of \nlake effect clouds and possibly some flurries or light snow \nshowers across northwest Indiana overnight and near the shore in\nnortheast Illinois Tuesday morning. The cold night and broader \nN/NNW gradient flow may keep most, if not all, potential showers\neast of the Illinois shore provided the existence of a modestly\nstrong land breeze.\n\nFocus remains on the potential for an active stretch of weather\nleading up to and on Thanksgiving. Guidance continues to \nexhibit a broad range of solutions owing to complex interactions\nof several central North American troughs (from Alberta to \nnorthern Mexico on Tuesday) as they congeal into a deep longwave\ntrough across the northeastern states by Friday. The exact \namount of moisture available for precip generation Wednesday \nnight also remains unclear as mid-level moisture sources from \nboth the central and tropical Pacific will need to align with \nlow-level Gulf and Atlantic moisture advecting into a very dry \nantecedent airmass. At this time, the clearest signals are that \n1) there will eventually be precip for much/all of the area on \nWednesday night into Thanksgiving and 2) very marginal \nthermodynamics (strong low-level WAA from the east) for snow \nmeans strong dynamic cooling would be the sole source of cold \nair for wintry precip. Accumulating snow is possible, but \nremains the less likely scenario at this time. However, any \ntrend toward a slightly colder and/or stronger system would \nquickly increase chances of accumulating snow away from Lake \nMichigan. Will continue to closely monitor this period for any \nnotable weather impacts heading into Thanksgiving.\n\nAnother lower-amplitude trough may bring precip, likely as all \nrain, across the western Great Lakes sometime this weekend, but \nconfidence this far out is very low given the unknown outcome of\nthe midweek weather.\n\nKluber",
